  "scraped_at": null,
  "content": {
    "title": "Venezuela rallies past Italy! Score, highlights from WBC semifinal",
    "text": "March 16, 2026Updated March 17, 2026, 12:31 a.m. ETIt's arrivederci, Italy, as Venezuela - boosted by a stellar bullpen performance - advanced to the World Baseball Classic Final against Team USA.Lifted by a three-run seventh inning, Team Venezuela defeated Team Italy, 4-2, in the WBC semifinal at Miami.Undefeated (5-0) entering Monday night, surprising Team Italy had handed Team USA its only loss on the way to this unanticipated run to the WBC semifinal.Down 2-0 after three innings, Venezuela held Italy in check with 7.2 scoreless innings by six relievers, ending with Chicago Cubs closer Daniel Palencia's electric 1-2-3 ninth inning.It's the first time in the six iterations of the WBC that Venezuela has advanced to the final, to be held Tuesday night, March 17, at Miami.New Jersey's ownEx-Yankees reliever Ron Marinaccio is now on for Italy in the ninth, trying to keep it a 4-2 Venezuela lead (which he does). Marinaccio, now with the San Diego Padres, is from Toms River.Mahwah's Kyle Teel, the White Sox promising young catcher, suffered a hamstring injury for Italy against Team USA, and is unfortunately sidelined for the next four-to-six weeks.Team Italy third baseman Andrew Fischer, from Brick, N.J., last year's first-round draft pick by Milwaukee, sports a Frank Sinatra tattoo on his arm.He's had a fine tournament but struck out his last two ABs after a walk and a single tonight.Emptying the tankWith no promise of tomorrow, Team Italy manager Francisco Cervelli's strategy was to go with big starters Aaron Nola and Michael Lorenzen back-to-back tonight - then worry about how to navigate Team USA in the final.Well, Lorenzen was one out away from holding a 2-1 lead when Team Venezuela struck with four straight singles, taking a 4-2 lead in the seventh.And that's where we are heading into the bottom of the eighth, with 5-6-7 coming up for Italy, with Andres Machado now pitching for Venezuela.Six outs awayVenezuela's bullpen continues its standout performance tonight, and in this WBC.The Seattle Mariners' Eduard Bazardo quickly retires 2-3-4 in Italy's lineup - including Jakob Marsee and Vinnie Pasquantino - to take a 4-2 lead into the eighth inning.Venezuela takes the lead, adds onOn a sharp, Maikel Garcia RBI single to left off Lorenzen, it is 3-2, Team Venezuela in the top of the seventh.And now the Team Italy nightmare inning continues as Lorenzen - who had looked great striking out both Wilyer Abreu and Willson Contreras on nasty changeups - yields an RBI single to Luis Arraez.It's 4-2, Venezuela, and they've chased Lorenzen from the game; Kyle Nicolas coming in for Italy.A chance for Ronald Acuna Jr.Runners at the corners, two out, seventh inning, Venezuela down 2-1. Let's see what happens in this matchup vs. Lorenzen......and it's a game-tying, infield hit. We've got a 2-2 game here in the top of the seventh.Keeping them in the ballgameVenezuela's bullpen has come up big so far, with the Royals' Luinder Avila (2.1 scoreless innings) yielding to ex-Kansas City teammate Angel Zerpa.Now with the Milwaukee Brewers, Zerpa strands the bases loaded in the sixth, and we go to the seventh - still 2-1, Italy.Eight-pitch sixth inningThat's helpful for Michael Lorenzen's longevity in this game.Lorenzen quickly retired Team Venezuela on three fly balls (vs. the heart of the order, including Eugenio Suarez) to maintain Team Italy's 2-1 lead as we go to the last of the sixth.Another Cervelli storyHad to look up the date, and some of the circumstances, but this was a memorable night – June 24, 2009 – in the Yankees’ last championship season.That's when Francisco Cervelli picked a great moment to hit his first MLB home run.As you might recall, the Yanks were going south (9 losses in their last 13 games) and they’re getting no-hit into the sixth inning at Atlanta.It was so bad, GM Brian Cashman flew in from New York and sat in on a hitters meeting.Manager Joe Girardi gets himself ejected, things are looking super bleak, and Cervelli changes the momentum with a totally unexpected home run.Of course, the Yanks won that night, starting a seven-game winning streak, and they move into first place, never looking back.Aaron Nola out, Michael Lorenzen inNice work by Nola but he reached his prescribed 60-pitch limit (59 pitches) and now it's Lorenzen pitching for Italy, holding a 2-1 lead to start the fifth.And nice work by Lorenzen, retiring the side after a leadoff, four-pitch walk to Willson Contreras.Eugenio Suarez puts Venezuela on the boardAnd just like that, Suarez connects off Nola for a one-out solo homer, cutting Italy's lead to 2-1 in the top of the fourth.How about that?In an NL East-battle within this WBC, Aaron Nola fights back in the count to strike out Ronald Acuna Jr.That helps to strand a runner at first and allows Italy to maintain its 2-0 lead moving into the last of the third inning. Nola is at 48 pitches, and you wonder if he's got just one more inning before handing the baton.Italy strikes firstIt's 2-0, Italy, after two innings, taking advantage of Venezuela starter Keider Montero's wildness (3 walks).Lefty Ricardo Sanchez, who last pitched (very briefly) in the majors with the 2020 St. Louis Cardinals gave up an inherited runner on a groundout, then got another groundout to end the frame.Something brewing for ItalyAnd it's not just the espresso. Bases loaded, one out in the second...Italy gets on the board after a single and three straight walks by righty starter Keider Montero, who is now done for the night.Montero has a 4.57 ERA over the last two MLB seasons, mostly as a starter, with the Detroit Tigers.Francisco Cervelli, take oneNo score, middle of the second.We'll try to add a few memories about Team Italy manager Francisco Cervelli, one of the most upbeat and genuinely nice guys to cover during his Yankees years.One of the rare down days for Cervelli was the last day of spring training in 2012, when he was all set to go north with the Yankees - only to be told he'd been optioned to Triple-A.The Yankees swung a last-minute trade with the San Francisco Giants, acquiring catcher Chris Stewart. Won't forget how stunned Cervelli was that afternoon at Steinbrenner Field.Clubhouse attendants are carting luggage out of the clubhouse, on the way to New York (or wherever the Yanks opened that year) and Cervelli was suddenly being re-routed to Scranton.Double plays to startEach teams gets a runner to first, and each is erased on an inning-ending DP to end the first inning.Aaron Nola draws the start for Italy, a late decision over the original choice, Michael Lorenzen, who might be following the Phillies righty tonight. Stay tuned.You'll recall, it was Lorenzen (now with the Colorado Rockies) who blanked Team USA over 4.2 innings in last week's upset win.",
